A||||6||FIEROCIOUS||Question for Fiero electricians (terryK)||||1||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||07-15-2000||11:53 PM||||I installed a Auto meter oil pressure gage in my Fiero and ran the tan wire from the rally gages to the tan wire on the oil pressure sender.<BR>   <BR>The original pressure sender had a sender and a switch to cut off power to the engine. when oil pressure is below 10 psi. power suplied by an orange and black wire leading to a tan white wire I took the switch out of line and connected hard wired it.<BR>now the fuel pump comes on with the battery connected!<BR>So how do I over ride this?<BR>I read that the engine will run without the switch? or wires connected.<BR>That the oil sender switch is a back up to the fuel relay?<BR>Is this correct? I just snip the wires and done?<BR>Terry? any clue?||||reg||1
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||07-16-2000||12:06 AM||||Here is a sketch that Mike made to help out <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/OILSEN~1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FIEROCIOUS||07-16-2000||12:32 AM||||Left is the old scematic<BR>Right is the current scematic<BR>As you can see the fuel pump is connected to constant power!<P>Thanks Chris (SCCA FIERO) for your help!||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||07-16-2000||12:35 AM||||You got it right when you said oil pressure sender (original equipment one) is a back-up to the fuel pump relay.  <P>The "switch" part of OE oil pressure sender doesn't kill the engine when there's no oil pressure.  Rather, it turns on the fuel pump whenever there's (practically) any oil pressure.  It works independent of the relay, and works whether or not ignition is turned on.  <P>How to override this?  One way is to remove the "hardwired" connection you put in, and let the relay do all the work.  If you don't trust the relay (and apparently GM didn't either), I suppose you could "tee" the OE sending unit into wherever you're picking up oil pressure for your aftermarket gage, and reinstall its connector.      <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 07-16-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000004||FIEROCIOUS||07-16-2000||12:08 PM||||Thanks, stugood.<BR>As far as the manuals explains it the oil sending switch was suppost to stop fuel supply if the engine dies or the event of a crash. <BR>The way this is set up that would never happen, the switches are in parallel! In order for it to work as GM intended it would have to be in series right? <BR>Anyways, I think I will run a switch to it, sort of a manual fuel pump switch incase the relay does go out. <BR>P.S. just for giggles could you use a relay from the headlights or some other sytem if you become stranded?<P>Thanks again all for your help!   ||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||07-16-2000||03:07 PM||||I can only speculate on GM's original design intent.  My <B>guess</B> is, they didn't want the fuel pump relay / control circuit to fail in the middle of nowhere, and leave you stranded.  The oil pressure switch would keep you going until you stopped for gas or whatever, and then when it wouldn't restart immmediately (have to wait for oil pressure), you'd become aware that something was amiss.<P>Like your manual switch idea  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> .<P>For the record, I don't know if I would want to try a horn relay in place of the F.P. relay, without investigating further.  Reason: it's controlled by electronics (the ECM).  When you change an inductive load (like a relay coil) that's controlled by an electronic circuit, without knowing more details, you risk frying the driving circuit.  It's also possible the relay coil is designed to operate on 5 volts.<P>Not saying that it wouldn't work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !  Just that it'd be too bad if the ECM got fried  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">, especially when your manual switch would be so simple and reliable.  As long as you don't forget and leave it turned on, of course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !       ||||reg||1
